1 NOTE {geni:about_me} I Salangen bygdebok står det at Karen var fosterpike. Måtte være fosterpike til John, da Gjertrud var hennes mor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Octogenarian Passed Away Monday Morning
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Karen Ranum, a Pioneer of Murray County Died Monday Morning, Caused from Old Age.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a long, busy, and useful life, Mrs. Karen Ranum, for many years a resident of Murray county passed away last Monday morning at the home of her daughter, Mrs. C.O. Amundson south of town, death resulting from old age.
2 CONT
2 CONT The deceased was born near Salanger (shall be Salangen), Norway on July 5, 1836. There she lived until she attained the age of womanhood, when she was united in marriage to Ole J. Ranum. Shortly after their marriage they immigrate
2 CONC d to America, arriving in Carver county in 1866. After a few years residence in Carver county they moved to Cottonwood county where they went thru the hardships and privations of pioneer and grasshopper times. In the early eightie
2 CONC s they moved to Murray county and took up a homestead near Chandler, where they resided until about 15 years ago when the husband passed away. Following the death of her husband, Mrs. Ranum, with her son, Ole, moved to this city a
2 CONC nd have since made their home here.
2 CONT
2 CONT A little over a year ago, the deceased became too feeble to keep house and for this reason made her home with her daughter, Mrs. Carl O. Amundson, who tenderly cared for her until she passed away.
2 CONT
2 CONT In her early years the deceased was baptized in the Lutheran faith to which she held steadfast and from which she received great solace. Hers was a pure, Christian life and as such she was ever thoughtful of the needs of others.
2 CONT
2 CONT She leaves to mourn her loss six children namely, Simon, of Alvin, Colorado, Gabe, Septer, and Iver of Lincoln. Nebraska, Ole, Jacob, and Mrs. C.O. Amundson of this city. Sixteen grandchildren and one great grand child also mourn 
2 CONC her departure.
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were held yesterday afternoon from the home at 1:00 o'clock and from the Lutheran church at 2:00 o'clock. Rev. Vordale officiating, after which the remains were laid to rest in the Slayton cemetery. 
